{
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "concern",
      "claim": "The spec declares risk: standard, but its touches list includes saffron/ledger.py, which is listed verbatim in .saffron/policy.yaml's elevate_on. saffron/repos/policy.py::effective_risk computes elevated whenever any changed file matches an elevate_on pattern, computed from the actual diff rather than from touches or the spec's declared risk. So this task will run at effective risk elevated regardless of what the frontmatter says, which makes the size gate blocking rather than advisory and marks the queue entry for a person to read cold rather than skim (DESIGN.md §5.6). This is very likely harmless here: the production diff is small (~20 lines, well under the 1300-token bug ceiling) and the new/expanded test files sit under the declared test_paths: [\"tests/**\"], so size_gate excludes their tokens from the count entirely (SA-0274). But the declared risk: standard is simply not the tier this attempt will actually run under, and a person approving this spec before it is built should know that up front.",
      "criterion": null,
      "file": null,
      "line": null,
      "fixes": null
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "claim": "DESIGN.md's mitigation table entry (\"Saffron breaks Saffron... Self-tasks are risk: elevated, put saffron/ in forbidden\") is stated as a blanket rule, but in practice (here and in the sibling specs SA-0334/SA-0335/SA-0337) self-tasks only elevate when they happen to touch one of the specific elevate_on globs, and forbidden only excludes the parts of saffron/ outside touches rather than the whole package. SA-0336 is the one of the four sibling specs that actually lands inside elevate_on (via saffron/ledger.py), which is what surfaces the concern above; this is worth noting only as context, not as a defect in this spec — the spec's own forbidden list is in fact about as tight as it can be given its touches.",
      "criterion": null,
      "file": null,
      "line": null,
      "fixes": null
    }
  ]
}
